**Current support and accepted design.** The Alpha compiler uses an optional `config.moth` with ordinary grouped `project` and active-builder records such as `html`. These named records remain the accepted configuration design. Build-input declarations currently use `#Config of T`; the queued directive plan replaces only that input spelling with `$config`. There are no `$project`, `$builder` or `$html_builder` directives. `entry_root` is retired. See @../progress (Progress) for implementation status.

`config.moth` is one self-contained compile-time source at the selected project root. It has no source dependencies, package resolution or authored file-value paths. A nested `config.moth` is inert to an enclosing build.

### Named project and builder records

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    project #= (
        name = "my_site",
        version = "1.0.0",
    )

    html #= ()
]

The `project` record is optional. An omitted name uses the selected directory name, and an omitted version means the project is unversioned. The active builder section follows its existing required/optional presence rule; HTML permits an omitted `html` record and applies its defaults. The selected directory is the source root.

Use an earlier `#Config of T` input (or `$config` when that migration lands) to provide a configurable value. A field is not itself an input declaration.

### Project record

The optional `project #= (...)` constant accepts these named fields:

[table:
    [th:
        [: Parameter]
        [: Type]
        [: Default]
        [: Configurable value permitted]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `name`]
        [: `String`]
        [: The selected directory name]
        [: No]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `version`]
        [: `String?`]
        [: `none`]
        [: Yes]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `author`]
        [: `String?`]
        [: `none`]
        [: Yes]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `license`]
        [: `String?`]
        [: `none`]
        [: Yes]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `template_const_loop_iteration_limit`]
        [: `I32`]
        [: The existing compiler-owned default]
        [: No]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `max_errors_per_module`]
        [: `I32?`]
        [: `5`]
        [: No]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `max_errors_per_project`]
        [: `I32?`]
        [: `30`]
        [: No]
    ]
]

The record's recognised settings schema is closed. Arbitrary fields, misspelled standard fields and `metadata = ...` are rejected. `@project` publishes the predefined fields except the two diagnostic limits. The diagnostic limits are compiler controls and are never published.

`name`, `template_const_loop_iteration_limit`, `max_errors_per_module` and `max_errors_per_project` remain fixed-only, including through helper constants. An omitted `name` defaults to the selected directory name and is validated as a Moth project identifier; it is never silently rewritten. Omitted `version` means unversioned.

### Diagnostic limits

`max_errors_per_module` is a strict allowance of primary errors retained for each module. File preparation keeps the first errors in source order up to the allowance. Ordinary-body diagnostic recovery admits a further eligible body after the first failed one only while the retained primary error count stays below the allowance. Check-only files attributed to one module share that module's allowance.

`max_errors_per_project` is a soft scheduling threshold for the whole command. Once the returned primary errors reach it, the compiler starts no further modules, source packages or check-only files. Work already running finishes and keeps its errors, so the project total may exceed the threshold. It is not a maximum result count. Skipped work is reported as not checked and never counts as successful.

A present value must be an `I32` from 1 to 2147483647. Zero, negative values and other types are rejected. Omitting a setting uses its default. An explicit `none` disables that one count and leaves the other unchanged. Warnings, notes and related labels never count. Neither setting disables the compiler's fixed recovery-work guard or changes program meaning.

### HTML builder settings record

An HTML directory project may use the named `html #= (...)` compile-time record. The command selects the builder first; this record never selects a backend or platform. Omitting the record applies its schema defaults.

The recognised setting names and defaults are:

[table:
    [th:
        [: Parameter]
        [: Type]
        [: Default]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `dev_output`]
        [: `String`]
        [: `"_dev"`]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `release_output`]
        [: `String`]
        [: `"_release"`]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `origin`]
        [: `String`]
        [: `"/"`]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `page_url_style`]
        [: `String`]
        [: `"trailing_slash"`]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `redirect_index_html`]
        [: `Bool`]
        [: `true`]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `html_lang`]
        [: `String`]
        [: `"en"`]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `html_title_prefix`]
        [: `String`]
        [: `""`]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `html_title_postfix`]
        [: `String`]
        [: `""`]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `html_favicon`]
        [: `String?`]
        [: `none`]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `html_inject_charset`]
        [: `Bool`]
        [: `true`]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `html_inject_viewport`]
        [: `Bool`]
        [: `true`]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `html_inject_color_scheme`]
        [: `Bool`]
        [: `true`]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `html_inject_core_css`]
        [: `Bool`]
        [: `true`]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `html_body_style`]
        [: `String`]
        [: `""`]
    ]
]

`page_url_style` accepts `trailing_slash`, `no_trailing_slash` or `ignore`. `html_favicon` accepts a compile-time String containing an untracked URL, not necessarily a string literal. Tracked file resources belong in a direct root builder slot because `config.moth` rejects authored file-value paths.

Names other than the selected builder's recognised settings section remain ordinary config constants; they do not implicitly configure another builder. The active settings record is checked against the selected builder's existing schema. Synthetic single-file operations without authored `config.moth` keep their explicit synthetic path and do not invent source settings.

### Example with typed inputs

Operational config uses declaration-owned `#Config` inputs and parenthesised grouped records:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    version #Config of String = "0.1.0"
    project_error_limit #I32? = none

    project #= (
        name = "moth_docs",
        version = version,
        max_errors_per_module = 5,
        max_errors_per_project = project_error_limit,
    )

    html #= ()
]

The `project` record may omit `name`, `version`, or both. An omitted name uses the selected directory name; an omitted version means unversioned. The record has no `entry_root`; the selected directory is the source root. The HTML section is optional and its schema defaults include `_dev` and `_release` outputs.

The grouped `project` record accepts `max_errors_per_module` and `max_errors_per_project` as fixed-only `I32?` compiler controls. Omission uses the default of 5 module errors or 30 project errors. An explicit `none` disables that one limit and leaves the other unchanged. A present value must be an exact `I32` from 1 to 2147483647. The grouped record has no field type context, so write an explicit absence through a typed `I32?` constant as the example does. Writing `none` directly in the record is a language type error. The loader validates and stores both settings today. The module limit governs file preparation and ordinary-body diagnostic recovery. The project threshold is a soft scheduling cutoff for new work in check, build and dev. When discovery ends early, one line after the diagnostics names the reason and says that later code was not checked. The CLI, the dev server's terminal and browser error page and the builder SDK's error text all show it.

Retired flat keys and `entry_root` remain rejected. Keep live `docs/config.moth` on this configuration model.

